{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_011CfpxJ8DkzBKXvwZz2BzWx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AQoVi9K2S5skMFcnwEBPnH","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"[REDACTED:local-path:03664a258ceb]/mnt/outputs/artifacts/deck-review-acmecorp/handoff/cw-dr-smoke-0a1b2c3d/checklist_output.json","content":"{
  "items": [
    {
      "id": "purpose_clear",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 1 states \"Acmecorp — AI-native developer tools for indie SaaS founders\" which names the category (developer tools) and the ICP (indie SaaS founders) but omits a quantified outcome. Sequoia's standard requires the format \"[Category] for [ICP] that delivers [quantified outcome]\" — the outcome dimension is absent from this tagline and from no other slide in the deck.",
      "notes": "Rewrite the slide 1 tagline to include a quantified outcome: for example, \"Acmecorp gives indie SaaS founders one CLI that replaces four tools and saves 20+ hours a week\" — this completes the Sequoia single-sentence format by adding what the product delivers."
    },
    {
      "id": "headlines_carry_story",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "Five of six slide headlines are single-word topic labels: \"Problem,\" \"Solution,\" \"Traction,\" \"Team,\" and \"Ask.\" Only slide 1 has a descriptive headline. DocSend and Sequoia both call for headlines that state conclusions an investor can read and retain without opening the slide body — a one-word label carries no conclusion.",
      "notes": "Rewrite every headline as a declarative conclusion: for example, \"Indie SaaS founders waste 20+ hours a week on tool integrations\" (slide 2), \"One CLI replaces GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel\" (slide 3), \"$8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth\" (slide 4), \"Two founders with inside knowledge of the stack\" (slide 5), and \"Raising $1.5M to reach $50K MRR in 20 months\" (slide 6)."
    },
    {
      "id": "narrative_arc_present",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The deck follows a partial Problem → Solution → Traction → Team → Ask arc. However, the seed-stage framework expects Problem → Solution → Why Now → Market → Traction → Business Model → Competition → GTM → Team → Ask. Six of those eleven slides are absent, leaving the arc with visible structural gaps. An investor finishing slide 3 (solution) has no sense of how large the market is, why now is the right moment, or whether the company can defend its position.",
      "notes": "Add the six missing slides in their standard positions before sending to investors: Why Now after Solution, Market after Why Now, Business Model and Competition after Traction, and GTM before Team. This completes the arc the seed stage requires."
    },
    {
      "id": "strongest_proof_early",
      "status": "pass",
      "evidence": "The traction slide (slide 4) presents the strongest proof in the deck: $8K MRR, 24 paying customers, and 18% MoM growth over the last 4 months. This appears on slide 4, within the DocSend threshold where 82% of readers who reach slide 4 finish the deck."
    },
    {
      "id": "story_stands_alone",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has six slides with an average of roughly 12–24 words each. The solution slide says only \"Acmecorp wraps GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel into one CLI\" — a reader with no verbal context cannot understand what the product does or how it changes the user's workflow. The team slide names no founders, shows no credentials beyond \"ex-Stripe (eng) and ex-Notion (product),\" and provides nothing a reader could act on. DocSend's share-deck standard requires that every slide be self-explanatory; this deck requires narration for at least four of its six slides.",
      "notes": "Expand each slide to be self-contained: add a one-sentence explanation of what the product does and shows on the solution slide, add full names and founder-market fit sentences on the team slide, and add context to the ask slide so a reader who has never met you understands what the capital buys."
    },
    {
      "id": "problem_quantified",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 2 states \"Indie SaaS founders waste 20+ hours/week on developer tooling integration.\" The ICP is specific and one dimension of pain is quantified (time). However, the cost of inaction in dollars, competitive exposure, or missed revenue is absent. There is also no customer evidence: no quotes, no discovery interview count, no design partner reference. Sequoia's problem framework requires quantifying across time, money, and risk — not just one dimension.",
      "notes": "Add a dollar or revenue cost alongside the time figure: for example, the average dollar spend across four separate tool subscriptions per month, or a milestone a founder missed because integrations broke. Then add one data point showing the problem was validated: \"12 founder interviews\" or a representative customer quote."
    },
    {
      "id": "solution_shows_workflow",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "Slide 3 states \"Acmecorp wraps GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel into one CLI.\" This is a feature description: what the product wraps, not what a user can now do that they could not before. There is no before → after workflow, no screenshot, no CLI command example, and no demo link. Sequoia's solution framework requires showing the workflow change; DocSend notes that investors in 2026 expect real product evidence even at seed.",
      "notes": "Replace or supplement the description with a 3-step before → after flow or a single CLI command block showing the workflow change. Even one terminal output example is more persuasive than a feature list. Add a demo link or animated GIF for investors who want to go deeper."
    },
    {
      "id": "why_now_has_catalyst",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no Why Now slide. The six slides cover company purpose, problem, solution, traction, team, and ask — none argues why this product is viable or urgent now. Sequoia asks explicitly: \"Why wasn't this built before?\" That question has credible answers for this product (proliferation of micro-SaaS founders post-2022, maturation of APIs from GitHub, Stripe, and Vercel, AI coding wave expanding the number of solo technical founders) but none are presented.",
      "notes": "Add a Why Now slide after the solution slide that names one or two genuine macro catalysts: for example, the sharp increase in indie SaaS founders since 2022, or the API maturity that makes CLI-level integration practical today where it was not three years ago. This is among the highest-leverage missing slides for a seed raise."
    },
    {
      "id": "market_bottom_up",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no market sizing slide. The six slides are company purpose, problem, solution, traction, team, and ask. Sequoia and YC both require a market slide; DocSend data shows the market section is among the most-read in funded decks. An investor reading this deck cannot estimate the addressable opportunity.",
      "notes": "Add a bottom-up market sizing slide: count the number of indie SaaS founders globally, multiply by a realistic willingness to pay per month for a CLI tooling product, and show the resulting ARR opportunity. A bottom-up calculation is far more credible to seed investors than a top-down \"the developer tools market is $NB\" assertion."
    },
    {
      "id": "competition_honest",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no competition slide. The six slides cover company purpose, problem, solution, traction, team, and ask. DocSend data shows investors spend 88% more time on the competition section in successful decks than in unsuccessful ones. The absence of any competitive framing leaves investors to fill in the map themselves, which rarely favors the founder.",
      "notes": "Add a competition slide that maps direct competitors (other developer CLI tools, workflow automation platforms), incumbents (the individual tool vendors: GitHub, Linear, Stripe, Vercel), and the do-nothing or build-it-yourself alternative. Include 2–3 concrete differentiators such as speed, breadth of integration, or pricing. \"No competition\" is never credible; an honest map shows market awareness."
    },
    {
      "id": "business_model_clear",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no business model slide. The six slides cover company purpose, problem, solution, traction, team, and ask. The deck states $8K MRR from 24 paying customers — implying a pricing structure exists — but that structure is never shown. Sequoia's framework requires the pricing model (seat, usage, per workflow), who pays versus who uses, and the gross margin structure, especially for AI-adjacent products.",
      "notes": "Add a business model slide showing: pricing structure (per seat, usage-based, or per workflow), who the buyer is (individual founder versus a company entity), and a gross margin estimate. For a product claiming to be AI-native, show how compute costs are managed and the path to healthy gross margins. This is also where you can show implied ARPU from the $8K MRR / 24 customers figures."
    },
    {
      "id": "gtm_has_proof",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no GTM slide. The six slides cover company purpose, problem, solution, traction, team, and ask. The deck shows 24 paying customers and $8K MRR — meaning a channel is already working — but never names it. Sequoia and DocSend both require ICP, channel, and early proof. Not naming how the current 24 customers were acquired is a missed opportunity at a stage where growth source matters as much as growth rate.",
      "notes": "Add a GTM slide that names the acquisition channel that produced the current 24 customers (direct outreach, community, content, product-led growth), provides a conversion rate or CAC estimate, and explains how this channel scales. For a developer-tools product, community-led or PLG is often the answer — name it and show the unit economics."
    },
    {
      "id": "team_has_depth",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "Slide 5 states \"2 co-founders, ex-Stripe (eng) and ex-Notion (product).\" There are no names, no photos, no LinkedIn links, no roles (CEO/CTO), no founder-market fit narrative, and no advisors or angels. DocSend data shows investors spend approximately 46 seconds on the team slide — about 30% of total attention. A slide with no names or faces gives investors nothing to verify, nothing to remember, and nothing to underwrite. The inventory confirms no visuals were present on slide 5.",
      "notes": "Add full names, headshots, and LinkedIn links for both founders. Write one sentence per founder explaining specific founder-market fit — not just where they worked, but why that experience makes them the right people to serve indie SaaS founders with this specific product. Name roles explicitly (CEO, CTO). If any angels or advisors have invested or agreed to advise, name them."
    },
    {
      "id": "stage_appropriate_structure",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The seed stage expected framework (from the stage profile) is: Company purpose, Problem, Solution, Why now, Market size, Traction/metrics, Business model, Competition, GTM, Team, Ask/use of funds — eleven slides. The deck has six: Company purpose, Problem, Solution, Traction, Team, Ask. Five of the eleven expected slides are absent: Why Now, Market size, Business model, Competition, and GTM. This is not a structural ordering issue; more than half the required content is missing.",
      "notes": "Build out the deck to cover all eleven seed-stage sections before raising. Prioritize Market size and Competition first — both are marked critical in the slide review — then Why Now, Business model, and GTM."
    },
    {
      "id": "stage_appropriate_traction",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Seed stage expects ARR/MRR, growth rate, and ideally retention data. The deck shows $8K MRR, 24 paying customers, and 18% MoM growth over the last 4 months — the right metric types for seed. However, there is no retention or churn figure, no NRR, no CAC, and no burn multiple. The absence of any retention data over a 4-month window leaves the durability of growth unaddressed. Carta and a16z guidance for seed in 2026 increasingly expects efficiency metrics alongside growth metrics.",
      "notes": "Add at minimum one retention data point on the traction slide: for example, \"0% logo churn in 4 months\" or \"average customer active for X months.\" Then add a burn multiple or monthly burn figure alongside revenue. These are the questions a seed investor will ask in the first meeting — the deck should answer them first."
    },
    {
      "id": "stage_appropriate_financials",
      "status": "warn",
